A 3D interactive globe that visualizes the hidden flows of the criminal economy:
money, goods, people, and digital activity across borders.
Instead of tourism or history, the app becomes a global illicit‑finance intelligence dashboard.

CORE EXPERIENCE
1. Global Illicit Financial Flows Layer
A dynamic overlay showing:
• Total estimated illicit flows by country
• Inbound vs outbound flows
• Hotspots of laundering, transit, and extraction
• Color‑coded risk intensity (heatmap)
Data animates as arcs between countries:
• Thickness = volume
• Color = typology (drugs, fraud, human trafficking, etc.)

2. Criminal Typology Breakdown
Each country or region displays a stacked profile of major illicit‑economy categories:
Estimated annual global values (based on your provided numbers):
• Drug Trafficking: $1.1 trillion
• Fraud & Bank Fraud: $579.4 billion
• Human Trafficking: $528.5 billion
• Cross‑Border Laundered Funds: $482.9 billion
• Terrorist Financing: $16.2 billion
Each category becomes a toggleable layer on the globe.

3. Flow Animations
When a user selects a typology:
• Drug trafficking: routes from production → transit → consumer markets
• Human trafficking: origin → transit → exploitation hubs
• Fraud: digital‑fraud hotspots, mule‑account corridors
• Terrorist financing: regional clusters (with disclaimers about extremist groups’ harm)
• Cross‑border laundering: offshore centers, secrecy jurisdictions, correspondent banking corridors
Arcs animate like a network map, showing direction and magnitude.

4. Country Intelligence Cards
Tap a country → see:
• Estimated illicit inflows/outflows
• Top criminal markets
• AML/CTF effectiveness score
• Known laundering typologies
• Offshore exposure
• High‑risk sectors (real estate, casinos, crypto, trade finance)

5. Time Slider
Drag from 2000 → 2026 to watch:
• Growth of drug markets
• Shifts in human‑trafficking routes
• Rise of digital fraud
• Expansion of offshore secrecy
• Terrorist‑financing patterns (with harm‑focused framing)

WHERE TO SOURCE ILLEGAL‑MONEY DATA (Legitimate, Public, Non‑Operational Sources)

These are safe, legal, open‑source intelligence (OSINT) sources used by analysts:
1. FATF (Financial Action Task Force)
• Mutual evaluation reports
• Typology reports
• High‑risk jurisdiction lists
2. UNODC (United Nations Office on Drugs & Crime)
• Drug trafficking routes
• Human trafficking reports
• Illicit‑flow estimates
3. World Bank & IMF
• IFF estimates
• AML/CTF effectiveness
• Trade‑based money laundering indicators
4. Global Financial Integrity (GFI)
• Trade misinvoicing
• Illicit financial flow estimates by country
5. Europol / Interpol
• Organized crime threat assessments
• Cross‑border criminal‑network analysis
6. Academic & NGO datasets
• Havocscope (black‑market data)
• Transparency International
• Basel AML Index
7. Public court filings & sanctions lists
• OFAC
• EU sanctions
• UK NCA
• DOJ indictments
These sources provide macro‑level intelligence, not operational details.
